in this video we are going to continue  our project by explaining the softmax  and cross-entropy concept which is  important for Mahler training from all  the training first we need to construct  a model instance using the model class  we defined before then we need to define  a loss function here we are using  cross-entropy  as the loss function that may sound  intimidating at first but don't worry  about it let's decode how to calculate  this cross entropy loss step by step  after getting the linear weighted sum  scores from the network we need to fit  it into a soft a max function softmax  function will normalize that the input  vector into the range from 0 to 1 which  can be interpreted as probability in  this way we can directly compare it to  the one hold included vector that  corresponds to your labels using cross  entropy this is the mass definition of  cross entropy notice that the lock  function can take zero however there is  a lot of zeroes in one hot encoding so  that the probability in the lock  function must come from the output of  the softmax function you can be assured  that the mathematically softmax function  will never produce 0 now let's see why  we want to optimize this cross entropy  loss for this one hot including vectors  where we have 1 when I equal to 3 and 0  for other classes the Koch entropy  function can be expanded where we only  have one term left where I equal to 3  our other term disappeared because of Li  equal to 0 so we only needs to focus on  one term on the model probability  outputs when I equal to 3 now if we want  to minimize cross entropy loss we need  to make log as term as small as possible  looking at the log function plot here as  needs to be very close to 1 in order to  follow Gaz to have a small value in such  a way by optimizing cross entropy loss  we can force the model to optimize these  parameters to make the right prediction  so that's the reason why we use cross  entropy as loss function here and in the  next 